  "tldr": "The project studies the unrestricted finite-population decision game for a fixed binary multiarm contrast with at least three active arms. Its theorem-level kernel is a design-independent, full-dimensional allocation-tube transfer proving that the normalized second-order improvement converges to a positive, finite decision value defined independently of the finite minimax risks, with total attaining procedures and matching lower priors uniform over dependent designs and labeled biased estimators. A finite response-alphabet linear program and directional Airy operator give the sharp benchmark \\(\\Lambda_c=C_A\\kappa(c)^{2/3}\\) and a total procedure under independent allocation \\(q_a^\\star=|c_a|/2\\). The unrestricted value is not assumed to equal that benchmark: an open equality-or-strict-loss theorem decides whether dependent assignment or local allocation correction improves it. Effective rational certificates and a sign-cell approximation theorem connect the limit to finite planned trials without assuming arbitrary real contrasts are computable.",
  "project_justification": {
    "gap": "Sudijono, Dobriban, and Tchetgen Tchetgen obtain an exact Airy second-order expansion only after a scalar two-arm reduction, and Hull's unrestricted bounded-outcome theorem is likewise binary-treatment. Existing multiarm design results are first order, restrict the estimator, change nature's class, or study regular central-limit behavior. The accepted parent reaches only the order \\(n^{-4/3}\\) and certificates at each fixed \\(n\\).",
    "niche": "With three or more active arms, the response alphabet has \\(2^J\\) faces, allocation perturbations at scale \\(n^{-1/6}\\) affect the target risk scale, and a scalar-path prior is exploitable by pair-focused designs. This creates a genuinely multivariate boundary decision problem in which labels and dependent count laws remain visible.",
    "fill": "The project derives the allocation-tube limit experiment from the exact orbit likelihood, proves uniform finite-to-limit upper and lower transfers for all procedures, evaluates the independent directional benchmark through a rational response-alphabet program and Airy spectral problem, and resolves the independent-versus-dependent design dichotomy with certified computation."
  },
  "related_work": "Sudijono, Dobriban, and Tchetgen Tchetgen (2026, Theorems 2.1--2.3, Theorem 3.1, Section 3, Appendix C, and the Conclusion) identify the unrestricted two-arm game with a scalar binomial experiment, derive its Airy correction by a discrete expansion, and match it with a squared-ground-state prior; the first changed proof step here is replacing their design-ancillary scalar prior by a full-dimensional response-type tube uniform over every allocation count. Hull (2026, Theorem 1, proof Steps 1--3, and the Conclusion) reaches the same two-arm finite game through coordinate swaps, separate convexity, and a least-favorable endpoint prior; his result motivates but does not settle multiarm independence. Armstrong (2022, Theorem 5.1 and Corollary 5.1) proves first-order efficiency bounds for broad sequential and multiple-treatment superpopulation designs, whereas the present target is a second-order fixed-schedule risk. Bai (2023, main minimax randomization theorem and the treatment-effect application) uses permutation invariance with linear schemes; its group averaging motivates unit symmetrization but not the unrestricted nonlinear converse. Aronow and Lopatto (2026, Theorems 1--3) separate marginal inclusion probabilities from pairwise dependence under unbiased total estimation; that separation motivates the count-law analysis while their estimator restriction prevents subsumption. Hirano and Porter (2009, Sections 2--4) show how a limit experiment can transfer decision-theoretic risk, but their smooth first-order framework does not control the present lattice boundary uniformly. Levit (1986) supplies the positive-solution spectral method adapted in the directional Airy calculation. Li and Ding (2017) and Mukerjee, Dasgupta, and Rubin (2018) establish regular multi-treatment finite-population asymptotics and contrast notation, not a nonregular unrestricted minimax boundary. Hammer et al. (1996) supplies the prospective regimen-trial consumer only; no claim reanalyzes its censored outcomes.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The exact finite \\(K=3,n=3\\) full-label risk \\(511653/4000000\\) is below the scalar Bayes lower value \\(18213/136000\\). This is a diagnostic against finite-sample scalar sufficiency, not evidence of an asymptotic gap. A scalar-path prior also fails as an unrestricted converse because a pair-focused design can exploit it. The proposal therefore requires a nonzero transverse tube and does not count the directional variational identity as the statistical limit theorem.",
  "honest_scope": "The response-alphabet linear program, its dual score, and the Airy upper construction are concrete proof targets, but the allocation-uniform Bayes bridge is the exposed open content. The project fixes \\(K\\) and \\(c\\), makes no claim with growing \\(K\\) or uniformly vanishing weights, and treats irrational contrasts only through a specified approximation sequence and a proved modulus. Under two active arms the construction must reduce to the published scalar Airy value. Under three or more active arms, equality of the unrestricted and independent second-order values remains answer-open until the allocation-tube game is solved.",
